  <p class="lede">
    Getform rebranded to Forminit in early 2026. If you've landed here
    looking for alternatives — or you're on Forminit and wondering what
    else is out there — this is a straight comparison of the form
    backends worth considering, with the tradeoffs each one actually
    carries.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rebrand-context">Quick context: what changed with the rebrand</h2>
  <p>
    Getform — a long-running form-backend service popular with static
    site builders — rebranded to Forminit in January 2026. The product,
    endpoints, and pricing largely carried over; the name and the
    marketing positioning didn't. If you've been on Getform for years,
    your forms still work. If you're shopping for a form backend today
    and seeing "Forminit" everywhere, that's why.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rebrand is a reasonable moment to take stock. Form backends are
    easy to switch — the only real lock-in is the endpoint URL — and the
    category has gotten more competitive in the last 18 months. Below is
    what's worth comparing, written by someone who builds in this space
    and reads the competitor landing pages weekly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disclosure">Disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    I build <a href="https://ollastack.com">Ollastack</a>, one of the
    services listed here. I've tried to write this fairly — naming where
    competitors genuinely win, including the cases where I'd recommend
    them over my own product. If something below feels slanted, email me
    and I'll fix it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-pick">How to think about picking one</h2>
  <p>
    Form backends compete on a small number of axes. The right one for
    you depends on which axis you weigh hardest.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Free-tier generosity</strong> — how many submissions per
      month you get without paying, and whether features (webhooks,
      exports, integrations) are gated behind paid plans.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dashboard and UX</strong> — how nice the inbox is to live
      in, especially if you'll spend time reviewing submissions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Developer ergonomics</strong> — clear errors, predictable
      behaviour, CLI or library support.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Programmatic submission</strong> — whether non-browser
      clients (scripts, mobile apps, AI agents) can submit cleanly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Email deliverability</strong> — notifications actually
      landing in your inbox, with Reply-To routed sensibly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Price at scale</strong> — what you'll pay at 10k or 100k
      submissions per month, including any per-submission overages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Agent-first API</strong> — whether the surface is designed
      for an AI agent to drive, not just a browser form post.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    No service wins on all of these. Pick the one that wins on the axes
    that hurt most.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alternatives">The alternatives, ranked by fit</h2>

  <h3>1. Formspree — the incumbent</h3>
  <p>
    Formspree is the oldest meaningful player in the category. They've
    been doing this since 2014, have the most integrations, the most
    documentation, the best React library (<code>@formspree/react</code>),
    and the most third-party tutorials. If you Google "how to add a
    contact form to my Astro site," you'll find a Formspree tutorial.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Formspree wins</strong>: maturity, framework libraries,
    integration breadth (Slack, Mailchimp, Stripe, Zapier, AWS, Webhooks,
    the lot), and the world's deepest documentation corpus. If you want
    the "boring choice that just works," this is it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Formspree loses</strong>: pricing climbs fast above
    the free tier (50 submissions/month on free), the dashboard
    aesthetic feels like it was designed in 2017, and there's no
    first-class story for non-browser submitters.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Formspree if</strong>: you want the most mature,
    most-documented option and don't mind paying for it past 50
    submissions a month.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Basin — the design-forward option</h3>
  <p>
    Basin (<code>usebasin.com</code>) positions explicitly toward
    designers and "build forms that work, without all the work." The
    dashboard is the prettiest in the category by some distance, the
    onboarding flow is excellent, and they've gone deep on visual polish
    in places competitors don't bother.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Basin wins</strong>: hands-down the nicest UI to live
    in, strong spam filtering out of the box, generous free tier (100
    submissions/month with most features), $8.25/mo starter plan that's
    competitive.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Basin loses</strong>: developer ergonomics are a
    secondary concern — the docs lean more on screenshots than code, and
    submissions from non-browser clients (scripts, agents) are a
    second-class path.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Basin if</strong>: you'll spend real time in the
    dashboard, you care about how it looks when you demo to a client,
    and your forms are filled by humans in browsers.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formcarry — the lifetime-archive option</h3>
  <p>
    Formcarry's pricing starts at $6/mo, but the differentiator is the
    lifetime submission archive on free. Most competitors cap free tier
    submissions or rotate them out; Formcarry keeps them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Formcarry wins</strong>: cheapest paid plan in the
    category, lifetime archive on free tier, CSV and JSON export with no
    plan gating, file uploads on most plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Formcarry loses</strong>: the product surface is
    thinner — fewer integrations, less polish — and the documentation is
    closer to "reference" than "tutorial."
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Formcarry if</strong>: price is the dominant axis and
    you want submissions archived forever without paying for retention.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Forminit (formerly Getform) — the static-site veteran</h3>
  <p>
    Getform-now-Forminit has been around for years and has a strong
    following in the Hugo/Eleventy/Jekyll communities. The rebrand
    modernized the marketing site and tightened the positioning; the
    underlying product is the same well-tested form backend it's always
    been.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Forminit wins</strong>: rock-solid reliability (years
    of operation behind it), strong static-site-generator integration
    guides, good Zapier and webhook support, file upload support on all
    paid plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Forminit loses</strong>: pricing is mid-pack ($9/mo
    starter, climbing to $24+), the dashboard is functional rather than
    delightful, and there's no first-class story for agent or automation
    traffic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Forminit if</strong>: you're already on Getform and the
    rebrand made you nervous — there's no reason to leave just because
    the name changed. The product is the same.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Web3Forms — the unlimited-free option</h3>
  <p>
    Web3Forms takes a different tack: unlimited submissions on the free
    tier, with most features open, monetizing via paid tiers that unlock
    dashboards and team features. If your form-submission volume is
    unpredictable and you don't want to think about quota, Web3Forms
    removes the worry.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Web3Forms wins</strong>: unlimited free submissions,
    simple pricing, fast setup, good for prototypes and side projects.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Web3Forms loses</strong>: no dashboard on free tier
    (you only get email notifications), less battle-tested deliverability
    than Formspree-class services, integration depth is thinner.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Web3Forms if</strong>: you want a free form backend for
    a personal site or prototype and the email-only-no-dashboard flow is
    fine for you.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Netlify Forms — the platform-native option</h3>
  <p>
    Netlify Forms is built into Netlify's hosting platform. If your site
    is already on Netlify, you can wire up a form by adding a single
    attribute to your HTML — no third-party service to sign up for.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Netlify Forms wins</strong>: zero-config when your
    site is on Netlify, included in their plans up to a quota, native
    integrations with Netlify Functions for custom logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Netlify Forms loses</strong>: locked to Netlify
    hosting (move and you rewrite your forms), and recent changes to
    credit-based billing have made the cost less predictable. Not an
    option if you're on Vercel, Cloudflare Pages, or anywhere else.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Netlify Forms if</strong>: you're already on Netlify
    and don't anticipate moving, and the included quota covers your
    volume.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Formspark — the lifetime-deal option</h3>
  <p>
    Formspark is the budget option with a different model: you pay once
    for a pool of submissions and use them at your own pace, no monthly
    subscription. A $25 one-time payment buys 50,000 submissions you can
    use over years.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Formspark wins</strong>: no recurring cost, simple
    mental model, great for low-traffic sites where a subscription feels
    overweight.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Formspark loses</strong>: feature surface is
    intentionally minimal, no first-class team features, less suited to
    anyone running multiple production sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Formspark if</strong>: you have one or two low-traffic
    sites and the idea of paying once for a multi-year submission budget
    appeals.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Ollastack — the agent-first option</h3>
  <p>
    Full disclosure (again): this is my product. I'll keep it short and
    let you evaluate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ollastack is a form backend built from day one with non-browser
    submitters as first-class users — scripts, mobile apps, and AI
    agents. Pricing is transparent and flat ($0 / $9 / $29), and every
    feature is available on every plan, including the free tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where Ollastack wins</strong>: cleanest path for scripted and
    agent submission, transparent flat pricing with no per-feature
    gating, every feature on the free tier (capped at submission
    volume), and built-in support for sending notifications from your
    own domain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pick Ollastack if</strong>: you're submitting forms from
    scripts, mobile apps, or AI agents; you value flat pricing over
    feature-gated tiers; you want every capability available on a free
    trial.
  </p>

