  <h2 id="migrate">How to actually migrate</h2>
  <p>Form backends are easy to switch. The work is mostly clerical.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Export your existing submissions</strong>. Every service
      on this list supports CSV export of historical submissions. Do
      this first, before changing anything — even if you don't need
      the data, you want it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign up for the new service and create your forms</strong>.
      Match field names so your downstream automations (webhooks, CRMs,
      Slack notifications) don't break.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Update the <code>action=</code> URL on every form</strong>.
      If you used an environment variable or a single source-of-truth
      constant, this is one change. If you hardcoded the endpoint in 50
      templates, this is 50 changes — keep a list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Test in a staging environment</strong>. Submit each form
      once, verify the notification arrives, verify webhooks fire,
      verify the data lands.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ship</strong>. Leave the old service active for a week in
      case anything routes there. Then revoke its keys.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>Total time for most sites: under an afternoon.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

  <h3>Is Forminit the same as Getform?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Getform rebranded to Forminit in January 2026. Existing
    endpoints, accounts, and submissions carried over. If you're an
    existing Getform customer, you don't need to do anything — your
    forms still work.
  </p>

  <h3>What's the best free form backend in 2026?</h3>
  <p>
    "Best" depends on what you weigh. Web3Forms wins on raw quota
    (unlimited submissions). Basin wins on free-tier UX. Ollastack gives
    you all features on free, capped at 100 submissions/month. Formspree
    is the safest "boring choice" with 50/mo. Pick based on the axis
    that matters to your use case, not the headline number.
  </p>

  <h3>Which form backend is best for AI agents?</h3>
  <p>
    Of the services here, Ollastack is built specifically for agent
    submission as a first-class case. The others can accept agent
    submissions, but you'll be fighting their spam defenses. We wrote
    about why this matters in
    <a href="/blog/form-backend-for-ai-agents">our post on form
    backends for AI agents</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Which form backend has the best deliverability?</h3>
  <p>
    Formspree, Basin, and Forminit are all mature on this axis — years
    of operation, established sending infrastructure, good Reply-To
    routing. Newer services (including ours) are catching up; if you're
    sending notifications to strict corporate inboxes, check whichever
    service you pick by sending a test submission to the destination
    before committing.
  </p>

  <h3>How much will I really pay at 10,000 submissions a month?</h3>
  <p>
    Roughly: Formspree $35-50/mo, Basin $25/mo, Formcarry $15-30/mo,
    Forminit $24/mo, Web3Forms $10/mo (with dashboard), Ollastack $29/mo
    (Team plan, no per-submission overage up to its cap). Verify each on
    the current pricing page — pricing changes more often than blog
    posts get updated.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaway">The takeaway</h2>
  <p>
    The form-backend category isn't a zero-sum fight. Different services
    optimize for different users — designers, static-site builders, side
    projects, enterprise teams, agent operators. The Forminit rebrand is
    a useful moment to look around, but most people land back where they
    started after looking, which is also a valid outcome.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're moving for a specific reason — pricing surprise, a feature
    gap, an integration that broke — name the reason explicitly before
    you shop. That's how you avoid switching just to switch.
  </p>
